{
 "esm": "1.2.0",
 "metadata": {
  "name": "NoahSoilMoisture",
  "description": "Soil water transport of WRF's unified Noah land-surface model as instantaneous tendencies: SUBROUTINES SMFLX, SRT and WDFCND of phys/module_sf_noahlsm.F (WRF 4.8, sf_surface_physics = 2), together with the canopy-interception budget and the three runoff terms. THE PDE is Richards' equation in mixed diffusivity/conductivity form, d(theta)/dt = d/dz( D(theta) d(theta)/dz - K(theta) ) - S, with Clapp-Hornberger closures D = dwsat (theta/smcmax)^(bexp+2) and K = dksat (theta/smcmax)^(2 bexp+3). It is written as the flux-form operator D(K_w D(sh2o) - F_w) and lowered by the SAME EarthSciDiscretizations rule the soil heat equation uses, column_nonuniform_1d/rules/varcoeff_face_flux_laplacian_lev -- NO NEW ESD RULE WAS NEEDED. That works because WRF evaluates the gravity drainage K(theta) UPWIND, at the layer above each interface, so from the operator's point of view it is a PRESCRIBED interface flux and drops straight into the rule's F slot; a scheme that averaged K across the face would not fit and would have needed a new rule. The same upwinding applies to the diffusivity (FORTRAN_BUGS N90, as in the heat equation). Orientation and geometry follow soil_heat.esm: `lev` is the soil layer index with layer 1 at the surface and `ze` the interface DEPTH below ground, positive downward. BOUNDARY FLUXES. The surface interface carries the infiltration pddum, and the bottom interface the free drainage slope wcnd[NLEV], which IS the subsurface runoff; both are prescribed, so the diffusive part at the two domain faces is correctly dropped. SINKS are the bare-soil evaporation, which withdraws from layer 1 only, and the per-layer transpiration. THE INFILTRATION LIMITER has a clean dt -> 0 limit that is not obvious from the Fortran: WRF forms infmax = px (ddt/(px + ddt))/dt with px = pcpdrp dt and ddt = dd (1 - exp(-kdt dt/86400)), and BOTH numerator terms are proportional to dt, so the limit is the harmonic-style combination pcpdrp r/(pcpdrp + r) with r = dd kdt/86400 a rate. dd is the column-integrated storage deficit, written here as an integral. The limiter is then floored at the surface conductivity and capped at the supply, and whatever the soil cannot take becomes surface runoff. DEW IS ROUTED LIKE RAIN: where the potential evaporation is negative the surface gains water, and NOPAC adds that gain to the precipitation before the canopy/throughfall split, so a dew step has a small positive canopy tendency and a small positive infiltration even with no precipitation at all. THE CANOPY is a reservoir of capacity cmcmax fed by shdfac prcp and drained by ec; what it cannot hold becomes drip, which joins the throughfall. In the dt -> 0 limit WRF's drip = max(cmc + dt rhsct - cmcmax, 0) becomes a SWITCH: zero while the canopy has room, and the whole net input once it is full, at which point dcmc_dt is zero. The reference set contains a step with the canopy exactly full and raining, so that branch is exercised. NOT TRANSCRIBED, and a consumer must not mount this where they matter: (a) SSTEP's supersaturation redistribution, which cascades water upward out of any layer driven past smcmax and reports the leftover as runoff3 -- that is a PROJECTION of the state applied after an integration step, not a rate, exactly like the slab scheme's snow cap, so it is reported as the indicator supersaturation_active instead; (b) the SMFLX predictor-corrector, which runs SRT/SSTEP twice on a half-updated column when the rainfall is heavy or the soil near saturation -- it is a finite-dt integration device and both branches converge to the SAME right-hand side as dt -> 0, so it does not affect this component, but it also means the corrector path is untested; (c) the frozen-ground infiltration reduction fcr, which IS written here because it is a clean closed form, but which the reference set never exercises (no soil ice anywhere), so it is UNTESTED rather than merely unused, as is every appearance of sice; (d) the WRF_HYDRO surface-head coupling. Consumer-supplied column fields (ze, sh2o, smc, et) are rewrite-target ops `input_<name>` lowered by the consumer, in the inline tests through per-test expression_template_imports libraries generated by tools/fill_tests.py.",
  "license": "MIT",
  "tags": [
   "wrf",
   "land-surface",
   "noah",
   "soil-moisture",
   "richards-equation",
   "runoff",
   "column",
   "pde"
  ],
  "references": [
   {
    "citation": "Clapp, R. B., and G. M. Hornberger (1978). Empirical equations for some soil hydraulic properties. Water Resour. Res., 14, 601-604."
   },
   {
    "citation": "Schaake, J. C., et al. (1996). Simple water balance model for estimating runoff at different spatial and temporal scales. J. Geophys. Res., 101(D3), 7461-7475."
   },
   {
    "citation": "Koren, V., et al. (1999). A parameterization of snowpack and frozen ground intended for NCEP weather and climate models. J. Geophys. Res., 104(D16), 19569-19585."
   },
   {
    "citation": "Chen, F., and J. Dudhia (2001). Coupling an advanced land surface-hydrology model with the Penn State-NCAR MM5 modeling system. Part I. Mon. Wea. Rev., 129, 569-585."
   },
   {
    "citation": "wrf-model/WRF v4.8.0 phys/module_sf_noahlsm.F SUBROUTINE SMFLX lines 2669-2813, SRT lines 3653-3947, SSTEP lines 3950-4077, WDFCND lines 4461-4519 (instrumented in ctessum-claude/WRF branch earthsciml-instrumented-chem170, commit 342156a3)."
   }
  ]
 },
 "metaparameters": {
  "NLEV": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 4,
   "description": "Number of soil layers, WRF num_soil_layers (4 for the Noah default). Bound into the column grid at the import edge."
  }
 },
 "models": {
  "NoahSoilMoisture": {
   "expression_template_imports": [
    {
     "ref": "${ESD_ROOT}/grids/column_nonuniform_1d/grid.esm",
     "bindings": {
      "NLEV": "NLEV"
     }
    },
    {
     "ref": "${ESD_ROOT}/grids/column_nonuniform_1d/rules/varcoeff_face_flux_laplacian_lev.esm",
     "bindings": {
      "NLEV": "NLEV"
     }
    },
    {
     "ref": "${ESD_ROOT}/grids/column_nonuniform_1d/rules/integral_lev_whole.esm",
     "bindings": {
      "NLEV": "NLEV"
     }
    }
   ],
   "subsystems": {
    "wrf": {
     "ref": "../../../lib/wrf_constants.esm"
    },
    "par": {
     "ref": "./noah_parameters.esm"
    }
   },
   "variables": {
    "ze": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "lev_nodes"
     ],
     "description": "Soil layer interface DEPTHS below the surface, positive downward, ze[1] = 0; the negative of WRF's ZSOIL prefixed by zero."
    },
    "zc": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "Soil layer centre depths, from the grid's column_cell_center template."
    },
    "dz": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "Soil layer thicknesses, from the grid's column_cell_thickness template. Equals WRF's SLDPTH."
    },
    "sh2o": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m^3/m^3",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "UNFROZEN volumetric soil water of each layer, WRF's SH2O. This is the transported quantity: Richards' equation moves liquid water only, and soil ice is an obstruction, not a participant."
    },
    "smc": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m^3/m^3",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "TOTAL volumetric soil moisture of each layer, WRF's SMC, read only to form the ice content smc - sh2o."
    },
    "et": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "W/m^2",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "Transpiration withdrawn from each soil layer, in energy units as the evapotranspiration component reports it. Converted internally to a water velocity."
    },
    "edir": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "W/m^2",
     "default": 30.0,
     "description": "Direct soil evaporation in energy units, which withdraws from the TOP LAYER ONLY."
    },
    "ec": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "W/m^2",
     "default": 50.0,
     "description": "Canopy-water evaporation in energy units, which drains the canopy reservoir and never touches the soil."
    },
    "prcp": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "kg/(m^2*s)",
     "default": 0.0,
     "description": "Precipitation rate reaching the surface, WRF's PRCP."
    },
    "shdfac": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 0.5,
     "description": "Green vegetation fraction, which splits the precipitation between canopy interception (shdfac) and direct throughfall (1 - shdfac)."
    },
    "cmc": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 0.0005,
     "description": "Canopy-intercepted water content, WRF's CMC."
    },
    "smcmax": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m^3/m^3",
     "default": 0.476,
     "description": "Soil porosity, SOILPARM.TBL MAXSMC."
    },
    "smcwlt": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m^3/m^3",
     "default": 0.084,
     "description": "Soil wilting point, SOILPARM.TBL WLTSMC, which sets the bottom of the storage deficit the infiltration limiter measures."
    },
    "bexp": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 5.33,
     "description": "Clapp-Hornberger b, SOILPARM.TBL BB."
    },
    "dksat": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m/s",
     "default": 2.81e-06,
     "description": "Saturated hydraulic conductivity, SOILPARM.TBL SATDK."
    },
    "dwsat": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m^2/s",
     "default": 2.39e-05,
     "description": "Saturated soil water diffusivity, SOILPARM.TBL SATDW."
    },
    "kdt": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 4.215,
     "description": "Infiltration parameter, refkdt dksat/refdk from REDPRM."
    },
    "frzx": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 0.1748,
     "description": "Per-soil-type frozen-ground parameter, frzk frzfact from REDPRM. Read only by the fcr reduction, which no state in the reference set reaches."
    },
    "slope": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 0.1,
     "description": "Bottom drainage-slope factor, GENPARM.TBL SLOPE_DATA: the fraction of the deepest layer's gravity drainage that actually leaves the column as subsurface runoff."
    },
    "prcp1": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Precipitation as a water velocity, prcp/rhowater."
    },
    "edir1": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Direct soil evaporation as a water velocity, edir/(lvh2o rhowater)."
    },
    "ec1": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Canopy evaporation as a water velocity."
    },
    "et1": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "Per-layer transpiration withdrawal as a water velocity."
    },
    "rhsct": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Net input to the canopy reservoir, shdfac prcp1 - ec1: interception minus evaporation, and negative when a dry-ish canopy is evaporating."
    },
    "canopy_full": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Indicator, 1 where the canopy reservoir is at or above capacity. WRF's drip test cmc + dt rhsct > cmcmax becomes exactly this switch as dt -> 0."
    },
    "drip": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Canopy drip: zero while the canopy has room, and the whole positive net input once it is full. The dt -> 0 limit of WRF's DRIP/dt."
    },
    "dcmc_dt": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Tendency of the canopy water content, rhsct - drip. Zero for a full canopy that is gaining, which is what makes the reservoir hold at cmcmax."
    },
    "pcpdrp": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Total water arriving at the soil surface, (1 - shdfac) prcp1 + drip."
    },
    "sice": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m^3/m^3",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "Volumetric soil ice of each layer, smc - sh2o. Zero everywhere in the present reference set."
    },
    "sicemax": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m^3/m^3",
     "description": "Largest soil ice content in the column, which throttles the diffusivity in WDFCND. Untested: zero throughout the reference set."
    },
    "factr2": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "Degree of saturation in liquid water, sh2o/smcmax, the argument of both Clapp-Hornberger closures."
    },
    "factr1": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "min(0.05/smcmax, factr2 of the layer), the reduced saturation used for the ice-blocked part of the diffusivity. Carried per layer below."
    },
    "vkwgt": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Ice weighting of the diffusivity, 1/(1 + (500 sicemax)^3): one for ice-free soil, falling towards zero as ice fills the pores."
    },
    "wdf": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m^2/s",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "Soil water diffusivity of each layer, dwsat factr2^(bexp+2), blended by vkwgt towards the ice-throttled form where soil ice is present."
    },
    "wcnd": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "Hydraulic conductivity of each layer, dksat factr2^(2 bexp + 3). This is the gravity drainage velocity."
    },
    "smcav": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m^3/m^3",
     "description": "Available pore space between wilting point and saturation, smcmax - smcwlt."
    },
    "dmax_density": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m^3/m^3",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "Per-layer storage deficit per unit depth, smcav - (sh2o + sice - smcwlt), i.e. how much more water this layer could hold. Integrating it over the column gives the depth of water the profile can still absorb."
    },
    "dd": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "description": "Column-integrated storage deficit, the depth of water the profile can still absorb."
    },
    "dice_density": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m^3/m^3",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "Soil ice per unit depth, the integrand of dice."
    },
    "dice": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "description": "Column-integrated soil ice depth, which gates the frozen-ground infiltration reduction at 1e-2 m."
    },
    "r_inf": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Storage-limited infiltration rate, dd kdt/86400. The 86400 is WRF converting its kdt, which is defined per day, into per second; it appears because the Fortran forms dt1 = dt/86400."
    },
    "infmax_storage": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Storage-limited infiltration capacity in the dt -> 0 limit, pcpdrp r_inf/(pcpdrp + r_inf): a harmonic-style combination that tends to the supply for a dry profile and to r_inf for a wet one."
    },
    "acrt": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Frozen-ground argument, 3 frzx/dice."
    },
    "fcr": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Frozen-ground infiltration reduction, 1 - exp(-acrt)(1 + acrt + acrt^2/2), and exactly 1 where the column ice depth is at or below 1e-2 m. The polynomial is WRF's CVFRZ = 3 factorial series written out. UNTESTED: no state in the reference set has soil ice."
    },
    "infmax": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Infiltration capacity: the storage limit reduced by fcr, floored at the surface layer's own conductivity (a soil can always take water at least as fast as it conducts it) and capped at the supply."
    },
    "pddum": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Water that actually enters the soil, min(pcpdrp, infmax). The surface boundary flux of the PDE."
    },
    "runoff1": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Surface runoff, the supply the soil could not take, max(pcpdrp - infmax, 0)."
    },
    "runoff2": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Subsurface runoff (baseflow), slope wcnd[NLEV]: the gravity drainage out of the bottom of the column, reduced by the slope factor. Note that WRF ADDS runoff3/dt to this before reporting it, so a consumer comparing against WRF's RUNOFF2 must add the supersaturation term this component does not produce."
    },
    "K_w": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m^2/s",
     "shape": [
      "lev_nodes"
     ],
     "description": "Soil water diffusivity at each interface, taken from the layer ABOVE it, K_w[kn] = wdf[kn-1] (FORTRAN_BUGS N90). The entries at the two domain faces are never read: the lowering takes the prescribed F_w there instead."
    },
    "F_w": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "shape": [
      "lev_nodes"
     ],
     "description": "Prescribed water flux at each interface, positive DOWNWARD: the infiltration pddum at kn = 1, the UPWIND gravity drainage wcnd[kn-1] in the interior, and the slope-reduced free drainage at kn = NLEV+1. Putting gravity here rather than in the diffusive term is what lets the existing face-flux rule serve Richards' equation unchanged."
    },
    "sink_density": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1/s",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "Water withdrawn from each layer per unit depth: transpiration everywhere, plus bare-soil evaporation in the top layer only."
    },
    "dsh2o_dt": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1/s",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "Instantaneous tendency of the unfrozen soil water, the flux divergence minus the withdrawals. The dt -> 0 limit of WRF's (SH2O_out - SH2O_in)/dt, whose finite-dt value differs because SSTEP solves the same operator implicitly."
    },
    "dsmc_dt": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1/s",
     "shape": [
      "lev"
     ],
     "description": "Instantaneous tendency of the TOTAL soil moisture. Equal to dsh2o_dt because nothing here freezes or melts: the phase-change sink lives in SNKSRC, which is not transcribed. A document that later adds the freezing branch must make these two differ."
    },
    "supersaturation_active": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Indicator, 1 where any layer is at or above smcmax and still gaining water, which is the condition under which SSTEP's untranscribed supersaturation redistribution would fire and produce runoff3. Zero throughout the reference set. A consumer that sees this go to one is outside what this component reproduces."
    },
    "etp": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "W/m^2",
     "default": 100.0,
     "description": "Potential evaporation in energy units from the Penman component. Read ONLY for its sign: where it is negative the surface is gaining dew, and NOPAC adds that gain to the precipitation reaching the canopy and the soil."
    },
    "dew": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Dew deposition rate, max(-etp, 0)/(lvh2o rhowater). WRF's NOPAC sets DEW = -ETP1 and then PRCP1 = PRCP1 + DEW on the etp <= 0 branch, so dew is routed exactly like rainfall: split between canopy and throughfall by shdfac. Missing this term leaves a small positive canopy tendency unexplained on every dew step."
    },
    "prcp1_eff": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Total liquid water supplied to the surface, prcp1 + dew_to_soil*dew + melt. This, not prcp1, is what the canopy and the soil see. The three terms are the three ways WRF gets water to the surface and they enter at the same point: rain (or, under a pack, rain that falls as rain), frost that NOPAC routes into the soil, and snowmelt that SNOPAC routes there. Verified against the reference: prcpf*0.001 + ex reproduces SNOPAC's own PRCP1 bitwise at all 67 snow-covered steps."
    },
    "melt": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m/s",
     "default": 0.0,
     "description": "Snowmelt water arriving at the surface, WRF's EX, which SNOPAC adds to PRCP1 before calling SMFLX. Zero on a snow-free column, where SFLX takes the NOPAC branch and there is no pack to melt. It joins the supply at exactly the same point as rain and therefore goes through the same canopy split; it is NOT a separate soil source. On a melting pack it dominates everything else: 8.0e-7 m/s at the strongest step of the cold-season set, against a bare-ground column's dew of order 1e-9."
    },
    "dew_to_soil": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 1.0,
     "description": "Whether dew and frost are routed into the SOIL water, 1 on a snow-free column and 0 under a snow pack. This is a real structural difference between WRF's two branches and not a switch of convenience: NOPAC does PRCP1 = PRCP1 + DEW, so frost on bare ground infiltrates, while SNOPAC sets DEW = -ETP1 and never adds it to PRCP1 -- the frost is deposited on the PACK and reaches the soil only later, as melt. Defaulting to 1 keeps every snow-free test unchanged; a consumer mounting this under a pack must set it to 0, and 14 of the 67 snow-covered steps of the cold set have etp < 0, so it is not a hypothetical."
    },
    "z_sfc": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "description": "Lower boundary of the column, ze[1]: the free name the EarthSciDiscretizations column_nonuniform_1d integral rules match as the lower bound of a bottom-anchored integral (ESD #39)."
    },
    "z_top": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "description": "Upper boundary of the column, ze[NLEV+1]: the free name the EarthSciDiscretizations column_nonuniform_1d integral rules match as the upper bound of a top-anchored integral (ESD #39)."
    }
   },
   "equations": [
    {
     "lhs": "ze",
     "rhs": {
      "op": "input_ze",
      "args": []
     }
    },
    {
     "lhs": "zc",
     "rhs": {
      "op": "apply_expression_template",
      "args": [],
      "name": "column_cell_center",
      "bindings": {
       "ze": "ze"
      }
     }
    },
    {
     "lhs": "dz",
     "rhs": {
      "op": "apply_expression_template",
      "args": [],
      "name": "column_cell_thickness",
      "bindings": {
       "ze": "ze"
      }
     }
    },
    {
     "lhs": "sh2o",
     "rhs": {
      "op": "input_sh2o",
      "args": []
     }
    },
    {
     "lhs": "smc",
     "rhs": {
      "op": "input_smc",
      "args": []
     }
    },
    {
     "lhs": "et",
     "rhs": {
      "op": "input_et",
      "args": []
     }
    },
    {
     "lhs": "prcp1",
     "rhs": {
      "op": "/",
      "args": [
       "prcp",
       "wrf.rhowater"
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "lhs": "edir1",
     "rhs": {
      "op": "/",
      "args": [
       "edir",
       {
        "op": "*",
        "args": [
         "par.lvh2o",
         "wrf.rhowater"
        ]
       }
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "lhs": "ec1",
     "rhs": {
      "op": "/",
      "args": [
       "ec",
       {
        "op": "*",
        "args": [
         "par.lvh2o",
         "wrf.rhowater"
        ]
       }
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "lhs": "et1",
     "rhs": {
      "op": "/",
      "args": [
       "et",
       {
        "op": "*",
        "args": [
         "par.lvh2o",
         "wrf.rhowater"
        ]
       }
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "lhs": "dew",
     "rhs": {
      "op": "/",
      "args": [
       {
        "op": "max",
        "args": [
         {
          "op": "-",
          "args": [
           0.0,
           "etp"
          ]
