    {
     "id": "chem170_scm_step2521_tendency",
     "description": "Reference: WRF 4.8 em_scm_xy single-column run at chem_opt = 170 with Noah (sf_surface_physics = 2, num_soil_layers = 4, silt loam, dryland cropland, slopetyp 1), data/eqwefic/chem170_scm, dumped by ctessum-claude/WRF branch earthsciml-instrumented-chem170 and replayed through the real64 (-fdefault-real-8) kernels/noah_driver. Inputs are the dumped SFLX arguments (real32 values widened). The column is unfrozen and unsaturated at every dumped step, so the frozen-ground infiltration reduction fcr and SSTEP's supersaturation redistribution are UNTESTED, not merely unused, and so is every path that reads sice. SOIL WATER AND CANOPY TENDENCIES at third-day midnight, the DEW step: etp < 0, so the only water input is dew, routed through the canopy exactly like rain. SSTEP advances the soil column IN PLACE with an implicit tridiagonal solve, so WRF's dt = 60 s increment is not the instantaneous tendency; the reference is the replay increment at ESM_DT = 0.01 s. drip and dcmc_dt are referenced at the SAME step and not at a smaller one, because WRF forms drip as (cmc + dt rhsct) - cmcmax: the increment is nine orders below cmc at dt = 1e-6 and is destroyed by cancellation, while at 1e-2 it is recovered to 1e-11. The per-level assertions carry an ABSOLUTE bound: the reference's own implicit-solve truncation is about 1e-11 (1/s), which is a few times 1e-5 of the DEEPEST layer's tendency, so the quiet layers cannot be pinned relatively at this step size while the top layer is pinned to 1e-7 of its own amplitude.",
