    {
     "id": "cold_A1_infiltration",
     "description": "Reference: WRF 4.8 em_scm_xy COLD-SEASON single-column run, data/eqwefic/noahcold_scm, dumped by ctessum-claude/WRF branch earthsciml-instrumented-noahcold and replayed through the real64 (-fdefault-real-8) kernels/noah_driver at ESM_DT = 1e-6 s. Same soil and vegetation as the warm run (silt loam, dryland cropland), so only the cold physics differs. These are the NOPAC steps -- bare frozen ground with no snow pack -- which is the regime this component describes; the SNOPAC steps are NOT used, because there the water reaching the soil is snowmelt routed through the pack rather than the prcp this component reads. WHAT THESE ADD over the warm tests: soil ice is present at every one of them, so the paths that read sice -- sicemax, the vkwgt ice weighting of the diffusivity, the sice term in the storage deficit, and the frozen-ground infiltration reduction fcr -- are exercised here for the first time. There is NO precipitation at any of these steps: the water supply is DEW, which NOPAC adds to prcp1 before the canopy split, so these tests also pin the dew routing on a second, independent set of columns. The soil-water TENDENCY is deliberately NOT asserted here. On frozen ground SFLX's SHFLX applies the phase-change sink SNKSRC to sh2o AFTER SMFLX has moved it, so (sh2o_out - sh2o_in)/dt is the sum of transport and freezing, and this component supplies only the transport; the frozen sink is a separate component that does not yet exist (FORTRAN_BUGS N95). Everything asserted below is computed in SMFLX BEFORE SHFLX runs and is therefore unaffected by it. Case A step 1: sicemax = 0.1435, dice = 0.1895 m, ABOVE the 1e-2 m threshold, so the frozen-ground reduction fcr is ACTIVE here -- the only step in the whole reference set that reaches it.",
     "parameter_overrides": {
      "edir": 0.0,
